          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a disorder of the whole joint, not just the cartilage. Cartilage is gradually lost, and the bone underneath changes in response. The joint lining, ligaments and surrounding muscles are also involved. It most often affects the knees, hips, hands and spine. (Source: \"Osteoarthritis,\" general clinical reference.)\n\nArthritis is the broader term. It means inflammation or disease of the joints. Osteoarthritis is one type. The source I searched does not compare the types in detail, so I can't tell you more about how they differ from it.\n\nThe same source notes that \"wear and tear\" is an inaccurate description. The process is active, involving inflammation and attempted repair. Because of this, the guidance treats exercise as the first-line approach, and resting the joint can make things worse.\n\nSome symptoms need prompt medical assessment rather than being treated as osteoarthritis. These include a hot, swollen, painful joint with fever, and sudden locking or giving way of a joint. Prolonged morning stiffness, or the same symptoms in several small joints, can point to a different type of arthritis. If you have any of these, please speak with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
